weYes. Based on everything we've traced, the next step should be to create one common reference for the Lambda execution role. I would do this before touching the policy template.

1. Stop making the IAM role name regional

In main.tf, you currently have something equivalent to:

iam_role_name = format(
  "%s-role%s",
  local.resource_default_name_env[local.env],
  local.resource_name_postfix
)

Change only the IAM role name so it doesn't use resource_name_postfix:

iam_role_name = format(
  "%s-role",
  local.resource_default_name_env[local.env]
)

Do not remove resource_name_postfix itself. KMS, S3, Lambda names, etc. can continue using -w2.

This means both regional deployments now agree on the name of the global role.

2. Make only East create/manage that role

In iam.tf, your current resource is roughly:

resource "aws_iam_role" "lambda_execution_role" {
  name = local.iam_role_name_final
  ...
}

Change it to:

resource "aws_iam_role" "lambda_execution_role" {
  count = local.is_primary ? 1 : 0

  name = local.iam_role_name_final

  # Keep the rest of your existing configuration unchanged.
}

Since you already defined:

is_primary = local.region == "us-east-1"

the behavior becomes:

us-east-1 → count = 1 → create/manage role
us-west-2 → count = 0 → DON'T create another role

3. In West, look up the existing role

Still in iam.tf, add:

data "aws_iam_role" "lambda_execution_role" {
  count = local.is_primary ? 0 : 1

  name = local.iam_role_name_final
}

Now:

East
  → aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0]

West
  → data.aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0]

4. Create one ARN that everybody uses

Add a local, preferably close to your other IAM locals:

lambda_execution_role_arn = local.is_primary
  ? aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0].arn
  : data.aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0].arn

I would also create the name because your IAM attachment currently uses the role name:

lambda_execution_role_name = local.is_primary
  ? aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0].name
  : data.aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role[0].name

Now the rest of the project doesn't need to know who created the role.

5. Replace the Lambda references

Your screenshots show things like:

use_existing_iam_role = true

existing_iam_role_arn =
  aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role.arn

For example in updateCreditLimit_lambda.tf and account_validator_lambda.tf.

Change those to:

existing_iam_role_arn = local.lambda_execution_role_arn

Do that for every occurrence of:

aws_iam_role.lambda_execution_role.arn

where the code simply needs the execution role ARN.

